WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.580
A cell extract holds thousands of proteins.

2
00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:09.720
Is one protein there, in which form?

3
00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:11.160
And how big is it?

4
00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:17.580
An antibody binds one protein, but in a mixture it cannot show size or form.

5
00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:22.060
The mixture must be sorted first, and the protein held still.

6
00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:26.160
Western blotting detects selected proteins in a mixture.

7
00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:30.550
Sorting starts with a gel and an electric field.

8
00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:37.090
DNA has a negatively charged backbone, so in agarose it runs toward the positive

9
00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:40.590
electrode. Agarose sorts fragments by size.

10
00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:45.890
For proteins, the extract is heated in SDS, a detergent.

11
00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:51.910
It unfolds each protein and coats it with negative charge, roughly in proportion to

12
00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:56.990
its length. So charge scales with weight, and the protein separates by size.

13
00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:02.500
Load the proteins into a polyacrylamide gel and apply the field.

14
00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:06.120
Small proteins travel farther than large ones.

15
00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:12.360
A standard of proteins of known weight, usually in the first and last lane, turns

16
00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.140
band position into size.

17
00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:19.820
Then the sorted proteins are moved onto a membrane by electrophoretic transfer.

18
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:22.340
Next comes blocking.

19
00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:27.920
Leftover binding sites on the membrane are covered with protein, so antibodies do

20
00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:29.540
not stick to the membrane itself.

21
00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:32.880
A primary antibody binds the target.

22
00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:35.640
Washing removes unbound antibody.

23
00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:40.140
A second antibody that recognises the first then binds it.

24
00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:43.220
It carries an enzyme that makes light or colour.

25
00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:47.360
Several can bind one primary, which amplifies a weak signal.

26
00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:51.593
The result is a membrane with bands in lanes.

27
00:01:52.553 --> 00:01:56.833
Read the band's position against the standard, and you get a size estimate.

28
00:01:57.773 --> 00:02:03.593
Its intensity gives amount, but uncalibrated intensity does not give an absolute

29
00:02:03.593 --> 00:02:09.573
mass. Say insulin-treated cells give a darker band with an antibody to one

30
00:02:09.573 --> 00:02:12.013
phosphorylated serine of Akt.

31
00:02:12.733 --> 00:02:15.653
Blot the same samples for total Akt.

32
00:02:16.293 --> 00:02:19.893
That shows whether darker meant more phosphate, or more protein.

33
00:02:20.817 --> 00:02:24.317
A band of the right size is not proof of identity.

34
00:02:25.117 --> 00:02:30.197
Include a known positive sample, and a sample lacking the target, for example a

35
00:02:30.197 --> 00:02:31.557
knockout or knockdown.

36
00:02:32.717 --> 00:02:37.797
Lanes differ in transfer and loading, and uneven transfer can change signal two to

37
00:02:37.797 --> 00:02:43.597
fourfold. Add a loading control, a stable protein or the total protein on the

38
00:02:43.597 --> 00:02:48.057
membrane. And stay in the linear range, where signal tracks amount.

39
00:02:48.057 --> 00:02:54.277
Overloaded abundant targets saturate, so equal bands may hide real differences.

40
00:02:55.250 --> 00:03:02.430
In 1979, Towbin, Staehelin and Gordon described transfer of proteins to

41
00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:04.990
nitrocellulose, with antibody detection.

42
00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:11.170
In their SDS gels the band pattern was kept, though transfer was not quantitative.

43
00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:19.790
In 1987, Hoffman, Brown and Kunkel used antibodies to find a protein of roughly 400

44
00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:27.310
kilodaltons. They saw none detectable in muscle from boys with DMD, or from mdx
mice.

45
00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:35.740
In 1998, Ferrell and Machleder analysed individual Xenopus oocytes, one by one.

46
00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:41.700
The response was an all-or-none switch, equivalent to a Hill coefficient of at

47
00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:46.060
least 35. Pooled eggs would hide that.

48
00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:52.060
Rule: a band gives size, and amount only within the linear range.

49
00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:56.140
A band of the right size is not proof of identity.
